        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Capture the pot by holding the lowest qualifying hand at showdown. A 4-card 'Badugi' (all different suits/ranks) is the highest tier of hand.",
          gameplay: '1. **First Betting Round**: Following the blinds. 2. **First Draw**: Players discard any number of cards (0-4) and receive replacements. 3. **Second Betting Round**. 4. **Second Draw**. 5. **Third Betting Round**. 6. **Third Draw**. 7. **Final Betting Round**. 8. **Showdown**.',
          keyRules: [
            'No Suits Rank: All suits are of equal value for winning purposes.',
            'Aces Low: The Ace is the best (lowest) card.',
            "The 'Nuts': The best hand is A-2-3-4 of four different suits.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Prioritize 'Badugi' potential. If you are dealt 3 cards of the same suit, you MUST discard two of them, leaving you with a very weak 2-card hand.",
          intermediate: "Snowing (Bluffing): In a triple draw game, you can 'Pat' (discard 0 cards) to make opponents think you already have a powerful Badugi, forcing them to fold or play defensively.",
          advanced: 'Range Analysis: If an opponent draws only 1 card on the first draw and then stands pat on the second, they almost certainly have a completed Badugi. If that Badugi is an 8-high or better, you should fold unless you are drawing to a 7-high or better.',
          tips: [
            "Don't fall in love with a King-high Badugi. It might be a 4-card hand, but it is easily beaten by a 3-card hand that happens to be A-2-3.",
            "Pay attention to 'Board Cards' (what you have discarded). If you've discarded three Aces, your chances of drawing an Ace-high Badugi are significantly reduced.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
